3. System Architecture

3.1 Module 1: Fluid–Tissue Domain Coupling

Components:

VAS Tissue Cell Network:Represents vascular tissue topology and cell‑level connectivity.

Biorheological Fluid Solver (MASA solver):Models non‑Newtonian fluid flow through tissue networks.

Multi‑Scale Implementation Bridge:Links macroscopic tissue and fluid behavior to voxel‑level atomic states.

MASA Voxel Solver:Hexagonal voxel grid representing local state variables (density, temperature, pressure).

Heat‑Flux Model:Captures energy transport across tissue and fluid domains.

Cosmic Density Field:Astrophysical density distribution used as an external field influencing voxel states.

This module produces a set of bio‑physical state vectors:

[ (q_{bio}, p_{bio}) ]

representing generalized coordinates and momenta for the fluid–tissue system.

3.2 Module 2: Hamiltonian Coupling & Cryptographic Engine

Composite Hamiltonian:

[ H = H_{bio}(q_{bio}, p_{bio}) + H_{astro}(q_{astro}, p_{astro}) + H_{interaction}(q_{bio}, q_{astro}) ]

Where:

(H_{bio}): vascular tissue and fluid Hamiltonian

(H_{astro}): astrophysical density/flux Hamiltonian (FITS cube derived)

(H_{interaction}): coupling term between bio and astro domains

State Vectors:

[ x = (q_{bio}, p_{bio}, q_{astro}, p_{astro}) ]

Port‑Hamiltonian Formulation:

[ \dot{x} = (J - R)\nabla H(x) + Bu ]

(J): interconnection matrix

(R): dissipation matrix

(B): input port matrix

(u): external forcing (e.g., flux variations, boundary conditions)

Chaotic Regime Modulation:Parameter sweeps and boundary conditions are tuned to drive the system into chaotic regimes, producing sensitive dependence on initial conditions.

Chaotic Key Generator:Chaotic attractor trajectories (e.g., Lorenz‑like structures) are sampled to generate time‑dependent cryptographic keys:

[ K(t) = f(\text{trajectory}(t), \lambda(t)) ]

3.3 Module 3: Integrity & Decryption

Lyapunov Divergence Monitor:

[ \lambda(t) = \frac{1}{t} \ln \frac{|\delta x(t)|}{|\delta x(0)|} ]

Positive (\lambda) indicates chaos. This monitor serves as an integrity metric: valid keys must originate from regimes exhibiting appropriate divergence characteristics.

Phase‑Space Stability & Chaotic Key Divergence:Phase‑space trajectories are analyzed for stability, attractor structure, and key evolution behavior.

Symplectic Leapfrog Integrator:A symplectic integrator is used to preserve Hamiltonian structure during numerical simulation, ensuring physically consistent evolution.

Physics‑Anchored Decryption:Decryption requires reconstruction of the relevant Hamiltonian regime and key evolution (K(t)), enforcing physics‑based provenance.

4. Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL)

Entropy Source:Chaotic attractor coordinates and divergence metrics from the coupled Hamiltonian system.

Key Derivation:Non‑deterministic key (K(t)) derived from:

